imited_by: LIM:leakage\n- auto_limited_by: LIM:low_measurement_reliability\n- auto_limited_by: LIM:small_sample\n- auto_has_risk: RISK:DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY\n- auto_has_risk: RISK:LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD\n- auto_has_risk: RISK:MEASURE_RELIABILITY\n- auto_has_risk: RISK:SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER"}, {"id": "FAM:SF-APPS-068", "score": null, "document": "# FAM:SF-APPS-068 | Study family SF-APPS-068: Possession Control Index incremental R2 over ORtg (PCI is DRtg proxy)\n- observed_research_question: Possession Control Index incremental R2 over ORtg (PCI is DRtg proxy)\n- auto_outcome_status: artifact\n- observed_hypothesis: PCI (halfcourt PPP + second-chance - TO - transition leakage) explains Net Rating beyond ORtg, Pace, and FGA/poss.\n- observed_result: A R2=0.763 OOS 0.761; C R2=0.902 OOS 0.919; dR2=+0.139. Offensive-control components add +0.005 R2 beyond ORtg. Net~ORtg+leakage R2=0.948. corr(leakage, DRtg)=+0.894. PCI univariate r=+0.863 vs ORtg +0.866.\n- auto_failure_mechanism: C_artifact (apparent effect produced by a construction, selection or labelling flaw)\n- auto_limited_by: LIM:leakage\n- auto_has_risk: RISK:LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD\n- auto_has_risk: RISK:MECHANICAL_FLOOR\n- observed_failure_evidence: Headline PCI gain is leakage tracking DRtg (+0.89); offensive-control dR2=+0.005. Baseline omitted defense by construction (Net=ORtg-DRtg).\n- observed_sample_and_power: n=120 team-seasons; OOS 2025-26 30 teams"}, {"id": "FAM:SF37", "score": null, "document": "# FAM:SF37 | Study family SF37: Possession-level SQ2 test: proxy engine on all RS games\n- observed_research_question: Possession-level SQ2 test: proxy engine on all RS games\n- auto_outcome_status: inconclusive\n- observed_hypothesis: A matchup-conditioned PT picker has possession-level signal at team-game level.\n- observed_result: [APPM-017] C gamma +0.0714 SE 0.0057 p=0.0000; B DiD +0.062 [0.051,0.073]; proxy agrees with production engine 13/79 = 16.5%.\n- observed_result: [CHT-024] A +0.047 PPP (p<10^-3); B +0.062 PPP; C +0.0714 PPP, n=16,921 cells, p<10^-4. Coverage: 9,853 RS pairs; 35.7% clear, 33.8% ambiguous, 30.4% no_rec.\n- auto_failure_mechanism: E_unclear (mechanism unclear from the records)\n- auto_limited_by: LIM:small_sample\n- auto_has_risk: RISK:LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD\n- auto_has_risk: RISK:SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER\n- observed_failure_evidence: [APPM-017] Proxy fidelity 16.5% (<70%); in-sample contamination of RS aggregates; iid SEs.\n- observed_sample_and_power: [APPM-017] n=16,921 cells\n- observed_verdict: [APPM-017] inconclusive"}], "n_results": 4}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "defensive suppression",
    "recommended defensive mix",
    "adherence to recommended strategy",
    "winning"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "measurement of recommended mix",
    "adherence metric",
    "outcome definition",
    "confounding by opponent strength",
    "sample size series level"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-003",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "Full-calibrated headline 69.8% (37/53); DRIVE-modal (banned-PT) comparisons 85.7% (12/14); clean comparisons 64.1% (25/39); high-confidence 64.3% (27/42). CUT recs 13/13; POST_UP 0/4. Uncalibrated archival: 51.7% (31/60)."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-018",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "Spearman rho=-0.0639, p=0.099717, n=665 (no clear full-rank signal). Kruskal-Wallis H=32.9723, p=2.7e-05. Mann-Whitney BEST 1.257 (n=56) vs AVOID 1.092 (n=160), U=5546.0, p=0.004018. Trigger accuracy rho=0.4525, n=121."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-068",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "A R2=0.763 OOS 0.761; C R2=0.902 OOS 0.919; dR2=+0.139. Offensive-control components add +0.005 R2 beyond ORtg. Net~ORtg+leakage R2=0.948. corr(leakage, DRtg)=+0.894. PCI univariate r=+0.863 vs ORtg +0.866."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF37",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "[APPM-017] C gamma +0.0714 SE 0.0057 p=0.0000; B DiD +0.062 [0.051,0.073]; proxy agrees with production engine 13/79 = 16.5%. [CHT-024] A +0.047 PPP (p<10^-3); B +0.062 PPP; C +0.0714 PPP, n=16,921 cells, p<10^-4. Coverage: 9,853 RS pairs; 35.7% clear, 33.8% ambiguous, 30.4% no_rec."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-003",
      "what_failed": "Hit rate test of engine top recommendation versus team modal play type",
      "why": "Artifact produced by lookahead leakage and labeling flaw; small sample limited power (n=53)"
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-018",
      "what_failed": "Ranked playsheet validation of recommended counter rankings",
      "why": "Inconclusive due to small sample, lookahead leakage, and low measurement reliability"
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-068",
      "what_failed": "Incremental R² test of Possession Control Index over ORtg",
      "why": "Artifact from mechanical floor: PCI was a proxy for DRtg, and leakage inflated apparent explanatory power"
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF37",
      "what_failed": "Possession-level test of proxy matchup-conditioned play-type picker",
      "why": "Inconclusive because proxy fidelity was low (16.5%), in-sample contamination leaked signals, and i.i.d. standard errors were used"
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "The engine's play-type recommendations have exhibited fragile signal in small-sample studies, often contaminated by lookahead leakage",
    "No prior study has directly tested whether adhering to a recommended mix predicts winning",
    "Multiple earlier validation studies were flagged as artifact or inconclusive"
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "That adherence to a suppression-conditional recommended mix predicts series or game outcomes",
    "That the engine's recommended mix is causally effective or even directionally correct outside of curated settings",
    "That a test of the question is impossible; rather it shows that careful design is required to avoid known pitfalls"
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The recommendation engine's quality is not yet validated, so a test of adherence mixes the engine's validity with adherence behaviour; the question is testable as a joint hypothesis but risks being uninterpretable if the engine is poor",
    "Using per-game adherence instead of per-series adherence would increase sample size from 54 to approximately 300 games, improving power",
    "Strict temporal separation is the only way to avoid lookahead leakage: the recommended mix must be defined using data that was available before the game in which adherence is measured"
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "Whether a stable suppression-conditional recommended mix can be estimated from regular season data, given that defensive strategies and personnel change",
    "The reliability of play-type classification and suppression detection in the derived possession data",
    "The extent to which adherence to a recommended mix is confounded by general team quality, injuries, or strategic adjustments outside the scope of the engine"
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "Without the historical record, a naive series-level correlation might have been attempted, risking lookahead artifacts and low power. The evidence of lookahead-induced artifacts in similar studies (SF-APPS-003, SF-APPS-068) and inconclusive small-sample results (SF-APPS-018) directly motivates a design that uses per-game analysis, out-of-sample recommendation generation, and pre-registered falsification."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.35,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
        "text": "Using any data from the same series (or future games) to define recommended mix would inflate apparent effect and produce an artifact"
      },
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "54 series with a binary outcome is underpowered; shifting to per-game analysis mitigates but does not eliminate power concerns"
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Defense suppression profiles and recommended mix rely on accurate play-type tagging and matchup assignments, which may be noisy"
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [
      "FAM:SF-APPS-003",
      "FAM:SF-APPS-018",
      "FAM:SF-APPS-068",
      "FAM:SF37"
    ],
    "summary": "No earlier study addressed whether adhering to a recommended mix predicts winning; previous work evaluated recommendation hit rates (artifact, inconclusive) and the statistical properties of the engine's components."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
      "text": "Defining the recommended mix using data from the same series or later games would create an apparent predictive relationship that is not real"
    },
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "A series-level binary-outcome test with 54 units has low power for realistic effect sizes; a per-game design with series-level clustering is needed"
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "The defense's suppression tendencies and the engine's recommended mix depend on play-type labels that may be inconsistently applied or sparsely observed"
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Adherence could be correlated with opponent defensive weakness, general team offensive skill, or strategic adjustments that are not captured by the engine's suppression model"
    },
    {
      "code": "DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY",
      "text": "Defensive strategies evolve across seasons and even within a playoff series, so a static suppression profile may misrepresent the true defensive context"
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.1,
    "rejected": 0.3,
    "untestable": 0.2,
    "artifact": 0.3,
    "uncertain": 0.1
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The effect of per-game adherence to the suppression-conditional recommended offensive mix on game win probability, conditional on opponent and context.",
    "unit": "Individual playoff game.",
    "sample": "All playoff games from 2022-23 through 2025-26 for which a defense suppression profile and recommended mix can be estimated using only data prior to that series (e.g., regular season of the same year and earlier playoff rounds), with exact dates fixed before analysis.",
    "power": "Approximately 300 games; logistic mixed-effects model with series-level random intercepts. A detectable increase in win probability of at least 5 percentage points per standard deviation increase in adherence at 80% power is plausible; exact power will be assessed via simulation prior to unblinding.",
    "controls": "Team offensive rating (prior regular season), opponent defensive rating, home/away indicator, days of rest, and a binary indicator for key injuries (from a pre-registered source).",
    "falsification": "1) Permute the recommended play-type list within each game and test whether adherence to a random list predicts outcome; 2) replace adherence with a metric of general offensive efficiency not conditional on suppression to ensure the effect is incremental to overall quality.",
    "multiplicity": "Single pre-registered adherence metric: proportion of offensive half-court possessions using a play type ranked in the top 3 of the recommended counter list, with possessions weighted equally.",
    "decision_rule": "One-sided test that the adherence coefficient is positive at α=0.025 (after a planned Bonferroni correction for any secondary metrics). The result will be interpreted only if the falsification checks do not suggest artifact.",
    "data_version": "Play-by-play and possession data snapshots as of 2026-06-16, using point-in-time logic to exclude information unavailable before each game. The exact code generating recommended mixes will be frozen before any predictive test."
  }
}

